  "lesson": "## The progression behind thousands of songs\n\n\n  Musicians number chords by which scale note they sit on, using Roman numerals: C is I, G is V, A minor is vi, F is IV. I–V–vi–IV is the pop progression: Let It Be, Don't Stop Believin', With or Without You, half the Top 40.\n\n\n  Why it works: I is home. V is the tension that pulls hardest back to home. vi is the sad cousin of I (it shares two of its three notes). IV is a gentle lift that sets up the return. Tension, release, colour, lift, repeat.\n\n\n  Right hand plays each chord broken into an arpeggio (one note at a time, root to octave). Left hand holds the root. Once it is clean, try playing the right hand as a block chord on beat 1 instead.\n",
